Since I'm a Sonic fan (PC only at least...) and I saw my friend playing it I thought I could give this game a try and holy cow it was worth it! It's like merge of Sonic and Megaman, but so different, Let me point out few reasons why you should get it:
- Perfect both for casuals and hardcores. Casual and Easy difficulty grat HP regen making the game much easier yet still enjoyable. Normal and Hard are so difficult, that you would be dying A LOT, and especialy near bosses. Thankfuly the game keeps restarting you from the checkpoint so you wouldn't need to do entire stages again;
- It has three completly different playstyles. Words cannot describe it, just watch the trailer;
- Seriously awesome boss fights. Even when I died over 30 times in last boss fights I still had fun... or maybe I'm just weird by having fun being stuck in a boss fights...;
- To me the story is very decent. I usually don't care about it (that's why I don't blame Sonic Team for their... ideas) but here I had a goal to see every damn cutscene;
- Collectibles! Who doesn't like them? And they actualy do something! You will unclock the music (which really stays in your head), voicelines and artwork! That's actualy what I found so far. Who knows what else might be there;
- Don't like the story or just want to play like hardcores in the 90's? Sure thing, get Classic mode and ignore every cutscene or story-related dialogues. (Don't do that please... It is so good);
Now many may wonder: Is this some sort of ripoff? Like some sort of random clone of Sonic or Megaman? NOPE. True, it was heavily inspired on Sonic (that's confirmed, didn't check about Megaman), but gameplay is completly different. How different you may ask? Just look at the trailer! Did you see Sonic driving a motorcycle?! Or maybe Megaman... uhhh... ok you got me there. The only Megaman I played was Megaman Powered Up for PSP but that doesn't change that Freedom Planet is different from it.
So there you have it. Freedom Planet is not a ripoff or clone of any sort, gives amazing experience, is suitable for every fan of platformers and that music! I must get sountrack soon.
Verdict: Buy! NOW! Or wait for sale... your choice but BUY IT!

Fun game that gets even better if you're a Star Wars fan and love the, now non-canon, expanded universe.
It's an old game but I still found it very enjoyable on this day. Here's a list of the things I think they did good vs the things that were not as good:
The positives:
- Plot;
- Gameplay: Force powers, laser guns, lightsaber, command line tweeks. This is, by far, the best thing the game has to offer. I think they acomplished to make you feel like a force-user and showing how strong one can be using it and wielding a lightsaber. The tweeks you can do on the command line are also fantastic and add tons to the lightsaber fights;
- Sound;
The negatives:
- Some of the levels' layout;
- Some of the puzzles;
Cool, old game, that I liked a lot.

Tl;dr: interesting ideas with some very annoying elements of gameplay.
The good:
* Time manimulation mechanics are one of the best things I ever seen in gaming - doing different tasks at the same time (game's time, no real time) is very entertaining, solving problems, optimizing run and then, at the end of level, seeing it done in real time is very impressive. Though, the most intense and enjoyable moments were boss battles - every boss is somewhat different and requires different tactics to beat, that makes time control shine: player must go back in time just enough to increase damage output, but do not much, because boss will change it's phase too fast which could waste previous soldiers lives (they will be shooting empty space). Well done.
* Runs great;
Tne neutral:
* Story is "meh" - did not fell it at all (but the ending was sweet :D );
* Music is ok (personal preference);
* Looks ok (personal preference);
The terrible:
* Commander is annoying - I just don't like his monologues and he is not entertaining at all;
* Controls - oh my, oh my. What. A. Mess. Yeah, I exaggerate a bit. They are just inconvenient:
- movement: everything is ok, no problem;
- it seems that, sometimes, game eats my inputs but do not show anything in game (very frustrating in platforming-heavy sections). I have never encountered anything like this before.
- aiming. Just missed oppurtunity. It is possible to use mouse for actions but not for AIMING. The only options are move at the same direction of shooting; move and shoot at fixed direction; stand and shoot at any direction. There is no independence like in twin - stick shooter. Didn't stop me from finishing the game however it was kinda "kick in the ****".
* Visual pollution: SOMETIMES there are a lot going on the screen and it is hard to see projectiles moving towards you.
Recommended on sale? INDEED.
Just use these 3 hours to decide if controls are bearable for you.
Also: collectables and hardmode for extended palytime.

This game is a prequel of the first game in the Blackwell series, and is fairly similar to it. The plot is similar, some characters are the same, and the gameplay in general is that of a nicely crafted adventure game. There are however some differences also, such as different art style, different dynamic between characters, and some adjustments to gameplay from the first game.
Without going into spoilers too much, here are pros and cons, according to me.
PROS:
Low price;
Really good story, better than the first game, in my opinion;
Interesting dialogue;
Better puzzles, didn't get stuck as much as the first one;
CONS:
Absolutely completely remarkably poor choice of achievements, some are impossible to find without the guide, some are downright annoying. Sure some are OK, but that is not an excuse for the two smoking achievements that have no purpose and do nothing except irritate the player who has to replay the game just because the girl smoked and extra smoke or did not smoke enough.
Less likable art style, art feels rushed and, well, amateurish. No character portraits, which is disappointing, especially compared to the first game.
Needed to play the game twice to get the commentary achievement, and then another time to get the smoking achievements (the girl smokes more when Joey is active and you are listening to the commentary). This is 3 times I had to play a game that does not change one bit from playthrough to playthrough. I find this completely unacceptable and poor game design (believe it or not, achievements are part of game design, and if you choose to include them, make sure they add to the game and not subtract from it).
I feel the need to type things in manually demands more from player, as in have a piece of paper and a pen on them when playing. I found this to be somewhat annoying, A game should not need to do that to a player.
Overall, I can only recommend this game because of the good story and puzzles.
